---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About Agent Buildprint"
  description="Agent Buildprint shows how reusable implementation packets help coding agents build with scope, contracts, and proof instead of vague prompts."
>
  <main class="why-deck about-page" aria-label="About Agent Buildprint">
    <section class="why-slide hero-slide about-hero" aria-labelledby="about-title">
      <div class="about-hero-grid">
        <div class="slide-copy">
          <p class="deck-kicker">Agent Buildprint</p>
          <h1 id="about-title">AI agents need build packets, not vague prompts.</h1>
          <p class="deck-lead">Buildprints turn repeatable software work into scoped, verifiable packets that coding agents can run. Less guessing, less drift, more proof.</p>
          <div class="why-actions">
            <a class="btn primary" href="#pain-journey">See the journey</a>
            <a class="btn" href="#rbac-example">RBAC example</a>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-proof-strip" aria-label="Buildprint value">
            <span><b>Scope</b> known before code</span>
            <span><b>Phases</b> ordered for agents</span>
            <span><b>Proof</b> checked before claims</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="pitch-visual" aria-label="Vague prompt converted into a Buildprint packet">
          <div class="prompt-card">
            <span>Loose prompt</span>
            <b>Add RBAC to this SaaS</b>
            <small>agent must infer repo, scope, contracts, order, and tests</small>
          </div>
          <div class="packet-engine" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>BUILDPRINT.md</span>
            <i></i>
            <b>packetized work</b>
            <i></i>
            <span>VERIFY.md</span>
          </div>
          <div class="proof-card">
            <span>Verified output</span>
            <b>roles, permissions, protected routes</b>
            <small>deny-by-default tests plus implementation receipt</small>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="pain-journey" class="why-slide pain-slide">
      <div class="slide-copy">
        <p class="slide-number">The pain</p>
        <h2>The agent is not weak. The handoff is.</h2>
        <p>Most AI coding work starts with an underspecified chat message. The model then has to discover the repo, invent missing contracts, choose an implementation path, and judge its own success.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="journey-compare" aria-label="Before and after Buildprints">
        <article class="journey-lane broken-lane">
          <div class="lane-head">
            <span>Without Buildprint</span>
            <b>Blind build</b>
          </div>
          <ol>
            <li><b>Idea</b><small>"Add RBAC"</small></li>
            <li><b>Guess</b><small>agent hunts for architecture</small></li>
            <li><b>Drift</b><small>scope changes while coding</small></li>
            <li><b>Claim</b><small>"done" without durable proof</small></li>
            <li><b>Rework</b><small>human finds the gaps</small></li>
          </ol>
        </article>

        <article class="journey-lane buildprint-lane">
          <div class="lane-head">
            <span>With Buildprint</span>
            <b>Packaged build</b>
          </div>
          <ol>
            <li><b>Packet</b><small>goal, non-goals, host context</small></li>
            <li><b>Map</b><small>contracts and touched surfaces</small></li>
            <li><b>Build</b><small>phases keep work ordered</small></li>
            <li><b>Verify</b><small>tests and receipt prove claims</small></li>
            <li><b>Reuse</b><small>packet improves after each run</small></li>
          </ol>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="why-slide category-slide">
      <div class="slide-copy wide">
        <p class="slide-number">Coding evolution</p>
        <h2>Coding agents are moving from prompts to reusable build packets.</h2>
        <p>The market keeps adding structure around the model. First we prompted. Then we engineered context. Then we gave agents skills. Buildprints package the whole implementation job: goal, context, phases, constraints, and proof.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="category-stack evolution-stack" aria-label="Coding agent evolution">
        <article><span>01</span><b>Prompting</b><small>tell the model what you want</small></article>
        <article><span>02</span><b>Context engineering</b><small>feed the right repo, docs, and state</small></article>
        <article><span>03</span><b>Skills</b><small>give agents reusable procedures and tools</small></article>
        <article class="active"><span>04</span><b>Buildprints</b><small>package the complete build with proof</small></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="rbac-example" class="why-slide rbac-slide">
      <div class="slide-copy">
        <p class="slide-number">Example journey</p>
        <h2>A founder asks for RBAC. The Buildprint turns it into a buildable packet.</h2>
        <p>The request stays human-simple, but the agent receives a concrete implementation path: assess the host app, define permissions, wire helpers, protect routes, and prove deny-by-default behavior.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rbac-board" aria-label="RBAC Buildprint flow">
        <div class="founder-request">
          <span>Founder request</span>
          <b>Add roles and permissions without breaking the app.</b>
        </div>
        <div class="rbac-packet">
          <span>RBAC Buildprint</span>
          <ul>
            <li>Host assessment</li>
            <li>Capability contract</li>
            <li>Integration phases</li>
            <li>Verification receipt</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="agent-run">
          <span>Agent run</span>
          <b>Map -> implement -> test -> report</b>
          <small>every step has a named checkpoint</small>
        </div>
        <div class="verified-result">
          <span>Proof</span>
          <b>Allow, deny, missing-role, and unknown-permission checks pass.</b>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="why-slide package-slide about-inside">
      <div class="slide-copy">
        <p class="slide-number">What the agent gets</p>
        <h2>The packet answers the questions an agent usually has to guess.</h2>
        <p>A Buildprint stays flexible about implementation details, but it fixes the important product, boundary, and proof decisions before coding starts.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="package-graphic" aria-label="Buildprint package anatomy">
        <div class="package-spine">
          <b>Buildprint</b>
          <span>agent-ready packet</span>
        </div>
        <div class="file-cascade" aria-label="Buildprint package files">
          <article><b>Start file</b><span>where the agent begins</span></article>
          <article><b>Spec</b><span>what must exist when finished</span></article>
          <article><b>Contracts</b><span>data, APIs, side effects, and boundaries</span></article>
          <article><b>Plan</b><span>the build order and stop points</span></article>
          <article><b>Checks</b><span>how the result gets verified</span></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="why-slide split-slide about-proof">
      <div class="slide-copy">
        <p class="slide-number">Why investors should care</p>
        <h2>The durable asset is not one generated patch. It is reusable implementation knowledge.</h2>
        <p>Buildprints make agent work comparable, repeatable, and improvable. The same packet can be run by different agents, against different hosts, with proof attached to every serious claim.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="proof-terminal" aria-label="Validation checklist">
        <p><span>Market</span> agent work needs packaging</p>
        <p class="ok"><span>Product</span> packets are reusable</p>
        <p><span>Quality</span> proof travels with the build</p>
        <p class="warn"><span>Moat</span> library improves with every run</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="why-slide market-slide about-final">
      <div class="slide-copy wide">
        <p class="slide-number">Bottom line</p>
        <h2>Prompts disappear into chat history. Buildprints become a library.</h2>
        <p>The product is not another coding agent. It is the missing handoff layer between human intent and agent execution.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="poster-grid" aria-label="Agent Buildprint use cases">
        <article><span>Build</span><b>features too important for vague prompts</b></article>
        <article><span>Map</span><b>turn working repos into reusable packets</b></article>
        <article><span>Compare</span><b>benchmark agents on the same brief</b></article>
      </div>
      <div class="why-actions final-actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/create/">Submit Buildprint</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
